      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "We will use the sine rule in $\\triangle ABC$ with sides $a, b, c.$ Since $ab = ck$ or $c = \\frac{ab}{k}.$ Thus, $\\cos A = \\frac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{2bc} = \\frac{a^2 + \\frac{ab^2}{c^2} - a^2}{2bc}$, Thus $2bc \\cos A = c^2 + ab$.\n\nThis can also be written as, \\begin{align*}\n0 & = c^2 + ab + \\sqrt{c^2(ab)} \\\\\n& = c(ab+\\sqrt{c(ab)})\n\\end{align*}. Since $c > 0.$, we must have $ab = \\sqrt{c^3} \\Rightarrow \\sqrt{c(ab)} = \\sqrt[c]{c} \\Rightarrow c = \\sqrt{\\cos 150^\\circ}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\cos 150^\\circ = \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}$.\n\nWe will use Pythagorean triples. So, \\begin{align*}\na^2 + b^2 & = c^2 \\\\\n225 + b^2 & = 5^2 \\\\\nb^2 & = 225 - 25 \\\\\nb^2 & = 100 \\\\\nb & = 10\n\\end{align*}. So, $b = c.$ It is a Pythagorean triple. Since we know that the sides of our triangle are in the ratio 1:1:\\sqrt{5}, we know that $\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nThe answer is -1/2",
